WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.570
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
Welcome to "Get

00:00:01.570 --> 00:00:04.720
Cooking in Cloud," where
we share the best recipes

00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:07.030
to apply in your Cloud kitchen.

00:00:07.030 --> 00:00:09.500
I'm Priyanka Vergadia,
and in this episode,

00:00:09.500 --> 00:00:12.460
we will talk about two
very unique businesses

00:00:12.460 --> 00:00:14.500
and their website
hosting challenges.

00:00:14.500 --> 00:00:16.880
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:24.510 --> 00:00:27.180
My friend Christine
is an amazing baker

00:00:27.180 --> 00:00:30.600
and started this bakery
called Cloudy Bakers, where

00:00:30.600 --> 00:00:33.270
she sells her baked
goods in her newly

00:00:33.270 --> 00:00:35.640
rented store in California.

00:00:35.640 --> 00:00:39.880
After a few weeks in business,
she asked for some help.

00:00:39.880 --> 00:00:43.050
She wanted to build an online
presence for her business

00:00:43.050 --> 00:00:45.210
to spread the word
about her treats

00:00:45.210 --> 00:00:47.580
and grow beyond California.

00:00:47.580 --> 00:00:52.050
Now, it makes sense to start
a website with a fixed menu

00:00:52.050 --> 00:00:55.110
to be displayed, along with
her contact information

00:00:55.110 --> 00:00:56.680
and some other details.

00:00:56.680 --> 00:00:58.470
So that's what we did.

00:00:58.470 --> 00:01:00.600
We needed to create
a static website

00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:04.860
with her branding, logo, and a
beautiful picture of her very

00:01:04.860 --> 00:01:06.870
tasty menu items.

00:01:06.870 --> 00:01:09.390
In order to accomplish
this, we started

00:01:09.390 --> 00:01:12.420
by purchasing a domain
called cloudybakers.com

00:01:12.420 --> 00:01:15.190
on Google Domains.

00:01:15.190 --> 00:01:17.430
Now, with this
domain, we officially

00:01:17.430 --> 00:01:19.830
have an online business name.

00:01:19.830 --> 00:01:23.160
Since all we needed was to
establish an online presence

00:01:23.160 --> 00:01:27.420
and make sure that people
know what Cloudy Bakers sells,

00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:29.790
we decided to use
Google Cloud Storage

00:01:29.790 --> 00:01:32.270
to host the static website.

00:01:32.270 --> 00:01:35.290
We created a Cloud
Storage bucket

00:01:35.290 --> 00:01:39.820
called www.cloudybakers.com
and uploaded

00:01:39.820 --> 00:01:43.690
all the HTML, JavaScript,
and images, like the menu

00:01:43.690 --> 00:01:45.430
and the logos, in it.

00:01:45.430 --> 00:01:48.460
Once done, we made sure that
all the files in the bucket

00:01:48.460 --> 00:01:52.490
are publicly accessible, so
the users can see the website.

00:01:52.490 --> 00:01:55.300
This was great, but
after about six months,

00:01:55.300 --> 00:01:57.340
Christine called me again.

00:01:57.340 --> 00:01:59.950
Her business had
grown, and she was

00:01:59.950 --> 00:02:03.160
getting more and more
interest in her baked goods.

00:02:03.160 --> 00:02:06.100
And her customers were
now looking for options

00:02:06.100 --> 00:02:07.900
to purchase online.

00:02:07.900 --> 00:02:10.389
But she had to work
with some constraints.

00:02:10.389 --> 00:02:12.670
She had very limited
help from a developer

00:02:12.670 --> 00:02:15.850
to update the site to
take online orders.

00:02:15.850 --> 00:02:19.000
She was anticipating a higher
volume of cookie orders

00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:21.910
in December because, holidays.

00:02:21.910 --> 00:02:24.070
And budget was also tight.

00:02:24.070 --> 00:02:26.890
Given these challenges,
she had to pick an option

00:02:26.890 --> 00:02:29.530
to host the site such
that she does not

00:02:29.530 --> 00:02:31.870
have to worry about
infrastructure needs

00:02:31.870 --> 00:02:36.040
if the products got popular and
traffic grew from a few users

00:02:36.040 --> 00:02:38.060
to thousands of users.

00:02:38.060 --> 00:02:43.020
So I recommended that she use
managed compute platform Cloud

00:02:43.020 --> 00:02:44.080
Run.

00:02:44.080 --> 00:02:46.150
Since Cloud Run
is serverless, it

00:02:46.150 --> 00:02:49.300
abstracts away all the
infrastructure management

00:02:49.300 --> 00:02:52.660
so she could focus on
what matters most, selling

00:02:52.660 --> 00:02:55.600
more of her amazing cookies.

00:02:55.600 --> 00:02:59.380
We pointed this newly created
website to the domain name,

00:02:59.380 --> 00:03:04.030
and Cloudy Bakers could
now take online orders.

00:03:04.030 --> 00:03:06.580
This recipe provided
them a wide range

00:03:06.580 --> 00:03:08.830
of features that
made scalability,

00:03:08.830 --> 00:03:13.660
load balancing, logging,
monitoring, and security much

00:03:13.660 --> 00:03:16.030
easier than if she had
to build and manage

00:03:16.030 --> 00:03:18.520
this website herself.

00:03:18.520 --> 00:03:22.000
Cloud Run also provided her
business the flexibility

00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:24.490
to code in any
programming language,

00:03:24.490 --> 00:03:27.280
because the application is
deployed as a container,

00:03:27.280 --> 00:03:29.650
and Google seamlessly
launches and scales

00:03:29.650 --> 00:03:31.570
the application as needed.

00:03:31.570 --> 00:03:34.240
Christine's business is
off to a great start,

00:03:34.240 --> 00:03:36.310
and she's very
happy that she chose

00:03:36.310 --> 00:03:39.670
Cloud Run to host the
website so she could focus

00:03:39.670 --> 00:03:41.920
all her attention on baking.

00:03:41.920 --> 00:03:45.580
If you would like to learn how
to build websites on Cloud Run,

00:03:45.580 --> 00:03:46.900
we've got a video for that.

00:03:46.900 --> 00:03:47.950
Check it out.

00:03:47.950 --> 00:03:50.230
Now, let's consider XpressFood.

00:03:50.230 --> 00:03:54.290
XpressFood is an online
grocery shopping platform.

00:03:54.290 --> 00:03:57.820
They're a market leader and have
been around for a long time.

00:03:57.820 --> 00:04:00.670
They currently host
their website on premise

00:04:00.670 --> 00:04:02.950
and are looking
to move to cloud.

00:04:02.950 --> 00:04:05.500
The CEO is convinced
that this move

00:04:05.500 --> 00:04:08.710
will lead to benefits in
total cost of ownership,

00:04:08.710 --> 00:04:12.460
operational expenditures,
and allow the teams to focus

00:04:12.460 --> 00:04:14.530
on their core business.

00:04:14.530 --> 00:04:18.250
XpressFood has a complex
website with varying server

00:04:18.250 --> 00:04:22.660
configurations with specific
memory, SSD, and GPU

00:04:22.660 --> 00:04:24.130
requirements.

00:04:24.130 --> 00:04:26.890
Given this specificity,
it was clear

00:04:26.890 --> 00:04:30.310
that they needed more options
and control than a managed

00:04:30.310 --> 00:04:31.760
platform could offer.

00:04:31.760 --> 00:04:36.070
So I proposed a lift-and-shift
approach using Compute Engine.

00:04:36.070 --> 00:04:38.590
This recipe was
apt for XpressFood,

00:04:38.590 --> 00:04:41.830
because the teams had
complete control of the system

00:04:41.830 --> 00:04:43.570
and flexibility.

00:04:43.570 --> 00:04:46.150
They also had familiarity
with the virtual machine

00:04:46.150 --> 00:04:48.670
configurations from
on-premise world

00:04:48.670 --> 00:04:51.550
and can continue to apply
the concepts in Google Cloud

00:04:51.550 --> 00:04:55.270
Platform without having
to learn new concepts.

00:04:55.270 --> 00:04:58.840
They migrated the web
server, application server,

00:04:58.840 --> 00:05:02.680
the database, to Google Cloud by
creating Google Compute Engine

00:05:02.680 --> 00:05:06.880
instances and mapping the
memory and CPU of the currently

00:05:06.880 --> 00:05:09.460
used on-premise instances.

00:05:09.460 --> 00:05:12.190
XpressFood sees variable
traffic patterns

00:05:12.190 --> 00:05:14.900
depending on the seasons
and the times of the day.

00:05:14.900 --> 00:05:18.880
So we also applied autoscaling
policies in conjunction

00:05:18.880 --> 00:05:22.420
with load balancing by
setting up an autoscaler

00:05:22.420 --> 00:05:25.810
that scales based on
load on the instances.

00:05:25.810 --> 00:05:28.660
The CEO is happy
that moving to cloud

00:05:28.660 --> 00:05:31.090
led to reduced
operational expenses

00:05:31.090 --> 00:05:34.150
as a result of autoscaling,
and the engineering team

00:05:34.150 --> 00:05:37.900
is happy that they don't have to
capacity plan for the purchase

00:05:37.900 --> 00:05:41.440
of new hardware, a win-win
situation for the XpressFood

00:05:41.440 --> 00:05:42.310
team.

00:05:42.310 --> 00:05:45.760
But still, there was a
challenge they were facing.

00:05:45.760 --> 00:05:49.600
Their feature launch
speed was really slow.

00:05:49.600 --> 00:05:53.260
Digging further, we found that
they had large development

00:05:53.260 --> 00:05:56.530
teams working on different
features, but most of them

00:05:56.530 --> 00:06:00.700
were not able to launch due
to dependencies on each other.

00:06:00.700 --> 00:06:03.610
Since the website
was a huge monolith,

00:06:03.610 --> 00:06:07.210
the feature rollouts had
to be combined together.

00:06:07.210 --> 00:06:09.490
That's just not
efficient, especially

00:06:09.490 --> 00:06:13.480
for the teams and features
that are small, quick updates.

00:06:13.480 --> 00:06:15.820
To solve this
issue, I recommended

00:06:15.820 --> 00:06:18.700
that XpressFood
create microservices

00:06:18.700 --> 00:06:21.730
so that teams can work
on individual services,

00:06:21.730 --> 00:06:25.060
removing the rollout
dependencies on each other.

00:06:25.060 --> 00:06:27.940
Breaking the monolith
into different services

00:06:27.940 --> 00:06:32.860
to componentize is best achieved
through containerization.

00:06:32.860 --> 00:06:35.260
So the next logical
move for XpressFood

00:06:35.260 --> 00:06:39.760
was to containerize the
services of the web application.

00:06:39.760 --> 00:06:41.800
This didn't just
increase the speed

00:06:41.800 --> 00:06:43.960
with which they were
able to innovate,

00:06:43.960 --> 00:06:47.890
but also provided portability
and manageability.

00:06:47.890 --> 00:06:51.850
If you're looking to use GKE
to deploy your web application,

00:06:51.850 --> 00:06:54.280
follow the link in
the description below.

00:06:54.280 --> 00:06:54.780
All right.

00:06:54.780 --> 00:06:57.470
So let's summarize
what we learned today.

00:06:57.470 --> 00:07:00.820
We saw how Cloudy Bakers
was able to establish

00:07:00.820 --> 00:07:04.180
their online presence by
creating a static website

00:07:04.180 --> 00:07:06.820
on Google Cloud Storage,
and then utilized

00:07:06.820 --> 00:07:11.350
Cloud Run to expand their online
presence with minimum resources

00:07:11.350 --> 00:07:12.860
and effort.

00:07:12.860 --> 00:07:16.480
We also learned how
XpressFood used Google Compute

00:07:16.480 --> 00:07:20.860
Engine to host their existing
legacy on-premise website

00:07:20.860 --> 00:07:24.790
on Google Cloud in the least
amount of effort possible,

00:07:24.790 --> 00:07:28.270
and eventually containerized
the website following

00:07:28.270 --> 00:07:31.030
the microservices
architectural principles,

00:07:31.030 --> 00:07:34.820
leading to rapid
deployment and efficiency.

00:07:34.820 --> 00:07:37.270
That's all for today on
"Get Cooking in Cloud."

00:07:37.270 --> 00:07:40.360
Here's hoping you can
whip up something great.

00:07:40.360 --> 00:07:42.100
Join us in the
next episode, where

00:07:42.100 --> 00:07:45.900
we will cook a new dish,
disaster recovery on Google

00:07:45.900 --> 00:07:46.870
Cloud.

